WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.440
Wes Anderson has long been interested in taking
meta-elements of storytelling and making them

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.470
part of the main narrative.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.890
Whether it’s using curtains as a framing
device, using aspect ratio to denote periods

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.620
in time, or a book for narrative structure,
for Wes, any part of the filmmaking process

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.739
seems to have potential to be brought explicitly
into the story.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:45.150
In Isle of Dogs he does this with translation.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.790
Subtitles are something we usually consider
to be part of the film’s meta.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:53.629
Like the posters, credits, or opening production
logos, they only exist to the audience as

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:58.830
a part of the information surrounding the
film, not as a part of the story itself.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:03.900
But Wes is hard coding subtitles, translation,
and the audience’s ability to understand

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.400
characters directly into the movie.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.579
This has it’s benefits and downsides, which
we’ll explore, but is this just a stylistic

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:22.069
quirk? Or if we dig below the showy exterior,
will we find actual thematic significance?

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.740
There’s an intrinsic perspective to a language
within a film that is relative to the audience.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.840
In Isle of Dogs Wes chooses to use that perspective to his advantage
within the story.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:51.279
In the film Japanese is spoken by most Human
characters, english is spoken by dogs, or

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.640
more accurately it’s stated that woofs and
barks are dubbed into english.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.020
English speaking audiences understand their
communication without visible translation,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.880
While most of the Japanese spoken in the movie
requires some kind of visible “in-movie”

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:05.400
translator.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.860
Placing us in the dog's shoes contextually

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:20.349
The audience literally has to try to infer
meaning based on the behavior, and tonality

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:24.959
of the characters speaking Japanese, just
like a dog would have to, when trying to understand

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.940
a human.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.520
It’s a brilliant way to create scenes where
the audience can suddenly and easily imagine

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.580
how a dog might feel, listening to a human
talk.

00:02:37.599 --> 00:02:42.379
I think this was obviously a significant part
of the western director’s motivation to

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:45.700
set the story within a foreign context.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.510
The alternative ways of creating this language
barrier between the dogs and humans would

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.799
be messy.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.300
The dogs could speak their own language of
bark and growls, and this could have been

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:56.480
subtitled.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.860
But that would do less to allow an immediate
identification with the dogs.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:12.130
The dogs could speak english and the characters
could speak some type of gibberish, but this

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:18.439
may be too goofy and require a suspense of
disbelief too difficult for a lot of viewers.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:22.800
The third option would be the Homeward Bound
option,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.340
where both animals and humans speak english, but ostensibly can’t understand
each other.

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:35.180
This is also a bit awkward if used for more
than a few brief scenes.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.170
Within the film, for Wes, the ability to understand
the other language, is linked closely to the

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.269
ability to empathize and advocate for the
dogs.

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:42.879
It’s not an accident that the character
that is one of the strongest advocates for

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:45.360
the dogs is a native English speaker.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.489
Speaking the same language as the dogs, is
symbolic of an ability to empathize with the dogs.

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:54.920
The Tracy character exists because she’s
a character that would speak english, the

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.810
same language the dogs speak.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:01.770
She’s there, not because an english speaking
audience needs that character, but because

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:06.880
she speaks english (the dogs language) to
the other members of the pro-dog resistance,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.450
symbolically taking a position of advocacy
on the dog’s behalf.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:19.900
Similarly, Atari has a device that allows
him to understand and communicate directly

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.209
with his dog.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:25.610
This creates a connection strong enough that
he’s the only dog owner that tries to find

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.540
his dog on trash island.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:41.110
I think a theme that’s buried here is that,
being able to understand another group makes

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.360
it harder or impossible to treat that group
inhumanely.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.729
The odd types of on-screen translation Wes
chooses to use may seem like a stylistic quirk

00:04:49.729 --> 00:04:50.729
at first glance.

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:54.700
But on closer inspection, there’s actually
a degree of realism embedded

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.720
in how he deploys translation.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.280
In situations where no translation would be
available,

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.780
the audience usually doesn’t get any.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.000
And in situations where we would have translation,
it is provided similarly

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.300
to how it would be in real life.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:13.160
When watching an international news conference,
translation usually comes in the form of a

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:18.199
real-time vocal translator, not subtitles.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:23.700
And when we’d be reading something in Japanese
if we could, we read subtitles in english.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.960
And when we’re hearing a transmission from
a young boy,

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.260
another young boy is used as a translator.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:40.300
While I think these techniques are clever
and unique, their unfortunate downside is

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.500
that they make the film’s perspective somewhat
anglo-centric.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.610
What’s uniquely odd about Isle of Dogs,
is that even though it’s set in Japan,

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.520
it’s created for an english speaking context.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.720
I was able to read quite a few translated
reviews from Japanese viewers, and while these

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:01.139
reviews were overall pretty positive, many
viewers noted the awkwardness and confusion

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:05.840
created in the process of translating the
movie back to Japanese.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.979
The Japanese dub of the version has both the
dogs and the Japanese characters speaking Japanese

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:24.220
which completely destroys this scene,
where the device is supposed to enable communication

00:06:24.229 --> 00:06:29.889
between the boy and his dog, they can seemingly
already understand each other in the dub version

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:32.540
since they both speak the same language.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:40.060
And the in-movie translators simply become
useless dead-weight for the film

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.300
when translating it back into Japanese.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:48.460
Use subtitles or a dub here, and the translator 
and you're translating Japanese into English and then back into Japanese

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:52.240
Making the translator characters completely redundant

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.380
or leave the english
untranslated and let Japanese audiences try

00:06:56.389 --> 00:07:01.200
to make out the Japanese lines in the background.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.259
Neither solution is particularly elegant.

00:07:04.259 --> 00:07:09.189
Because the Japanese lines are designed not
to be understood in the film by non-Japanese speakers

00:07:09.189 --> 00:07:14.479
Wes felt free to leave some of those
lines muffled, or unintelligible.

00:07:14.479 --> 00:07:18.949
This left some Japanese viewers straining
to understand lines that were never intended

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:20.539
to be understood.

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:24.659
And while all the Japanese characters quickly
flashing across the screen are just background

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.880
noise for other audiences, many Japanese viewers
reported

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:31.360
being distracted by trying to read everything.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.200
The film’s thematic use of language is preserved
a little better for foreign releases where

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:44.090
the english can be dubbed. And the Japanese translated in a similar way to how it's translated in the English version

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:53.259
But because many of the English subtitles
are often a part of the actual physical animation,

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:57.319
changing them for every language would be
nearly impossible, and so in foreign versions

00:07:57.319 --> 00:08:02.810
of the film, much of the text that exists
as a part of the world is left untranslated.

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:06.569
These translations often aren’t vital to
the story, but not having them does detract

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:08.259
from the experience.

00:08:08.259 --> 00:08:12.979
But should the limitations of a technique
keep it from being implemented at all?

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:17.939
Should Wes have used different methods to
try to construct the perspective of the film?

00:08:17.939 --> 00:08:22.659
As we discussed, every way of giving the dogs
a language the audience can understand carried

00:08:22.659 --> 00:08:24.379
some downside.

00:08:24.379 --> 00:08:29.569
And any film is imbedded in a cultural context,
for some films, like Isle of Dogs, that context

00:08:29.569 --> 00:08:31.639
might be more integral to the story.

00:08:31.639 --> 00:08:36.910
I, an english speaking American will never
be able to watch Japanese films in quite the

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:38.680
same way a native speaker would.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:43.710
While a good movie can easily overcome the
limitations of experiencing it through subtitles

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:47.440
or (cringe) dubs, it does alter the experience.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.830
And even if I learned the Japanese I still
wouldn’t have the same cultural context

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:54.150
a native speaker would have.

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:58.700
But should that stop filmmakers from using
that context to help tell stories in inventive

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.380
and interesting ways?

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:04.860
Isle of Dogs is an interesting case study
of this issue, because it doesn’t just use

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:09.080
another culture arbitrarily, it does so in
service of it’s story,

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:11.920
but in doing so it makes itself more anglo-centric.

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:15.610
I’m curious to know what you think in the
comments.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:23.080
I don't think you can make any movie of a
cultural perspective, no matter what you do,

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:27.160
But should filmmakers try to minimize the perspective's impact on the story?

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.780
Or is it ok to use that perspective as a storytelling tool?

